lar  in the following centuries  especially in the eastern parts of the  muslim world  places like central asia and the indian  subcontinent  while baha'i is the main figure and  founder of the order  it is said that it wasn't consolidated  into a distinct tariqa until under the  influence  of a later follower called allah  was a second generation disciple of  nakshpant himself  and he was very important in the  establishment of the nakshpandiya as a  proper  order at tariqah the nakshbandiya is  quite unique  in terms of its chain of authority of  spiritual authority  you see all sufi orders and really all  sufi masters  trace their spiritual authority in a  chain called the silsila  that always eventually go back to the  prophet muhammad himself  so if you take basically any sufi sheikh  today like  for example the current leader of the  nakshpandi khalid  he received his jazza which is his  authority to teach and  spiritual instruction by his master  nazim al-haqqani  who received his spiritual transmission  of spiritual authority  from his master sheikh abdullah  al-ristani  and so on in a chain of masters that  eventually reached the prophet muhammad  himself  now all sufi orders and all sufi masters  have a chain like this  simply the only difference being that  they have different  variations on this chain with different  masters and different places and so on  but  every sufi order has a sidzilla a chain  of spiritual authority and transmission  the uniqueness of the nakshpandiya is  firstly that they have two main stems or  chains one of which goes through  ali the prophet's son-in-law and cousin  and this is probably one of the most  widespread or common ways for these  chains to work and  in this way ali is very important for  most of the sufi orders as being one of  the main persons  that traces this chain back to the  prophet muhammad but the nakshmandia  have  another chain that goes through the  first caliph abu bakr  before reaching the prophet muham