in case you didn't know this already  religions  are complex and the deeper you die the  more you realize what you have yet to  learn  even in a tradition like sufism which in  itself  is a part of the larger religion of  islam we quickly realized that it is  consists of a huge inner diversity and  complexity  not all sufis practice in the same way  and they don't believe in the same  things  in fact sufism is very famously divided  up into different  mystical orders in arabic nurse turok in  plural or  tariqa path in singular now not all  sufis adhered to a  specific sufi order and the order  structure was not the standard during  the first few centuries of its  development but from the 12th century or  so the  the the way of structuring the sufi or  surfacing into these different  mystical orders became the norm and  immensely popular  and remain so until today in this video  which is a collaboration with the  excellent historical channel al  mukhadema  we will be exploring one of the most  famous and widespread of these orders  and mochadema's video on the history of  islamic presence in the region of  qingyang  in china gives us some of the historical  context but  in this companion video we will be in  particular  diving into the tariqah called  nakshpandiya  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  the nakshbandiya or nakshbandi order is  an old one dating back to the  late middle ages it is widespread across  the world today and has often been  considered one of the more  orthodox sufi orders as we will see why  soon but if you watched any of my  previous videos you should know by now  that any discussions about things like  orthodoxy is often pretty useless the  origins of this tariqa is usually  considered to be the central asian sufi  saint and master baha'i  muhammad al-nakshband who was also the  namesake of the order itself  being from modern-day uzbekistan and  living in the 14th century  the order that was founded around his  teaching would become immensely popular  in the following centuries  especially in the eastern parts of the  muslim world  places like central asia and the indian  subcontinent  while baha'i is the main figure and  founder of the order  it is said that it wasn't consolidated  into a distinct tariqa until under the  influence  of a later follower called allah  was a second generation disciple of  nakshpant himself  and he was very important in the  establishment of the nakshpandiya as a  proper  order at tariqah the nakshbandiya is  quite unique  in terms of its chain of authority of  spiritual authority  you see all sufi orders and really all  sufi masters  trace their spiritual authority in a  chain called the silsila  that always eventually go back to the  prophet muhammad himself  so if you take basically any sufi sheikh  today like  for example the current leader of the  nakshpandi khalid  he received his jazza which is his  authority to teach and  spiritual instruction by his master  nazim al-haqqani  who received his spiritual transmission  of spiritual authority  from his master sheikh abdullah  al-ristani  and so on in a chain of masters that  eventually reached the prophet muhammad  himself  now all sufi orders and all sufi masters  have a chain like this  simply the only difference being that  they have different  variations on this chain with different  masters and different places and so on  but  every sufi order has a sidzilla a chain  of spiritual authority and transmission  the uniqueness of the nakshpandiya is  firstly that they have two main stems or  chains one of which goes through  ali the prophet's son-in-law and cousin  and this is probably one of the most  widespread or common ways for these  chains to work and  in this way ali is very important for  most of the sufi orders as being one of  the main persons  that traces this chain back to the  prophet muhammad but the nakshmandia  have  another chain that goes through the  first caliph abu bakr  before reaching the prophet muhammad and  this chain is often referred to  as the golden chain and it's the one  that most  nakshpandi masters emphasized the most  but in any case the nakshpandiya in all  of its inner diversity and  just like all other sufi orders has  certain characteristic practices and  teachings  that distinguish it from other orders  now it will be a mistake to think that  these different sufi orders or sufism in  general  has a distinct theology of its own in a  distinct  way for the most part sufis and history  and in these different sufioris have  followed one of the  established schools of kalam or theology  like the  asharis or the maturities for example  even if they were often also  hostile to speculative theology in  general instead what distinguishes these  different  sufi orders from each other and from  non-sufis is primarily  their practices now as i said the the  word for these orders in arabic is  tariqa which literally means a  path so they could be seen as different  paths different  methods and techniques to reach intimacy  with god also sometimes called  union with god and perhaps the most  characteristic and distinguishing  feature of the nakshbandi path  is their practice of silent vicar now  the good is probably the main  practice in all of sufism it literally  translates to remembrance and is a  practice in which  god is invoked through recalling his  different names or through phrases like  there is no god but god this is in fact  a practice that  basically all sufi orders and sufis  generally take part in in some way  but it can take on many different forms  it is  usually chanted rhythmically in a group  and on occasion even different  instruments like frame drums can be used  but the nakshbandia is unique in the  sense that their practice of the vikr is  entirely  silent that is it is not chanted out  loud but only in the heart  or mind of the practitioner the  justification for this is usually that  it will prevent the person from being  caught  preoccupied with the movement of the  tongue instead of being engrossed  in directing the heart towards union  with god  on top of this the nakshpandiya has also  generally rejected the use of  music as any kind of spiritual practice  there are of course many other different  aspects that are unique to the  nakshpandiya that we could discuss but  we don't really have time to go through  all of it in this video  there is for example the so-called 11  nakshbandi principles  supposedly authored by the founders of  the order and i'll leave them on the  screen so you can pause and read it  through if you want  but as of right now i will only be  exploring one of these principles which  is also i think a very characteristic  aspect of the nakshpandi order  and this is the idea of solitude in the  crowd this idea can briefly be  summarized as an attitude where  the mystic is to be internally detached  from the world  and only preoccupied with god while  outwardly  he or she still participate actively in  society  in other words the nakshbandi sufis have  generally  rejected the more kind of extreme forms  of renunciation that we find in many  other orders like the  shishtiya or the khalwatiya for example  there is no living alone in a cave in  the mountains in the nakshpandi order  generally at least this has also led to  the fact that nakshbandis have often  taken a reactive role in social and  political questions  in central asia and in india for example  they were often very close  with the emperors or the kings and  influenced the political  scene to a very large degree now the  nakshmander is not the only tariq or  order that has this kind of active  attitude of course but  it can still be said to be a very  characteristic aspect of their  path like i would say at least and  because of all of you above the  nakshpandiya it is often considered a  sober sufi order as it doesn't contain  many of the  other sort of ecstatic practices and  teachings that we find elsewhere  this active attitude of the order is  also probably one of the reasons that it  has been so successful in its wider  spread  as i've said previously they were  especially popular in central asia  and perhaps most prominently in on the  indian subcontinent  during mughal times for example the  nakshbandiya  rose to great prominence and often  influenced the politics and the court of  immorals  to a large degree in the indian context  as well as elsewhere  the nakshpandiya has often come to be  seen as representing  islamic orthodoxy and as being the  orthodox  sufi order of course statements like  this are incredibly problematic as terms  like orthodoxy are  always fluid and subjective but what it  does point to  is that the nakshbandis have often had a  more strict and conservative way of  applying or relating to the sharia the  islamic law  as well as its general sufi practices  in mughal india for example it often  stood in great contrast to the other  major orders like the shishtiya and  khadiriya  on a number of grounds the nakshbandis  as we have seen rejected any form of  sama or  music as a spiritual practice something  that was  widespread in both the shishti and  khandaria  they also performed this silent wikked  as opposed to the more vocal form which  was more prevalent  and the nakshbandi masters often openly  criticized the other orders for  not adhering properly to islamic law as  a result  they came to be seen by their supporters  as guardians of the islamic law  against the innovations of the heterodox  sufis  of course it's important to remember  that the supporters of the other orders  saw themselves in the exact same way  but when scholars talk about the  nakshbandiya today  this is the common image of a more  orthodox  sufi order that adhered strictly to  islamic law  in fact one of the most prominent and  influential figures in the history of  the nakshpandi order  appears in this very context in mughal  india  in the person of ahmed sir hindi he is  seen by many as a mujad a reviver of the  religion  and wrote a treatise he called  al-maktubat  where he openly criticizes some of the  other sufis in india  and their practices and ideas he  wanted to revive the religion and sufism  by returning it to a strict adherence to  the sunnah of the prophet muhammad  one of the main aspects of sufism in  india that he criticized  was the doctrine called or the unity of  being  so here we actually do get into a bit of  theology in general as i've said the  sufis follow the established schools of  theology  but they are distinguished from  non-sufis in their insistence on the  imminence of god in  the world and the possibility of  reaching intimacy with him  in this life the goal of much of sufi  practice throughout all of sufism  can be said to be the concept known as  fana which means annihilation this is  the  annihilation of the individual self into  the absolute reality that is god  and this concept has been understood in  many different ways throughout history  but generally we are usually not dealing  with  the idea that the individual is actually  united or  merged with god this mos sufis would  reject this idea  instead we are talking about a  realization of the non-existence of the  individual  self and thus a realization that god is  really the only thing that exists in  reality  the doctrine of which i already  dedicated several videos to  is usually traced back to the andalusian  sufi ibn arabi  and his school in criminally short terms  it is the idea that god is the only  reality  being identified with being or existence  itself  in arabic would which is thus ultimately  one  hence the unity of being ahmed sidhindi  was very critical of this idea  as he equated it with pantheism and  associated it with  hindu ideas so instead he proposed a  modification of this doctrine  which he called which means the unity of  the witnessing  the distinction here is that the  experience of uniting with god  is only a subjective experience that  does  not mean that it is objective a truth or  objective reality as such  thus trying to maintain the absolute  separation between god and creation  but now as many scholars like william  chidik have pointed out the whole idea  is really based on a misunderstanding of  ibn arabis doctrine  and when we dive deeper we realize that  they are essentially saying the same  thing  this is one perspective on it at least  and i will probably be dedicating a  whole other video to this question in  particular  so for now let's return to the main  subject of the video ahmed sidhindi's  ideas were so influential and  and popular that a whole branch one of  the largest branches of the order  was formed around him called the  nakshbandi mujadidi he and his followers  also had a significant influence in the  mughal court  whereas royals like akbar shah jahan  jahangir and  had been primarily influenced by the  shishti and khadiri orders  the emperor aurangzeb and many of his  successors  were nakshbandis and the rule is often  considered to have  inaugurated a more strict and  unforgiving period in mughal rule  which reflects the nakshpandi's more  kind of strict and conservative approach  to suvism and islam generally but the  order also spread outside of india and  central asia  it eventually made its way to china for  example which is a subject that  al-qaeda deals with in his video it also  spread to  anatolia and the ottoman empire and the  sort of egypt and then damascus syria  iraq and all  it eventually became an incredibly wide  spread  order which remains until today it is  one of the most widespread sufi orders  in the world at this point  there are major branches of the  nakshpandia in places like the united  states and  europe as well as in asia and the middle  east the nakshbandi haqqani order is  especially popular in the west and has a  strong presence online  to some degree the nakshpandi are still  seen as one of the more orthodox sufi  orders because many of their doctrines  and  characteristics that have been discussed  in this video but there's also a wide  span  of different branches and groups that  can strongly differ from each other  ranging from very traditional orthodox  forms  to more some of the more prominent  neo-sufi so-called movements in the  world today  the nakshbandi order is thus a very  complex tariqa  it originated in central asia and what  is today uzbekistan  but has since spread across most parts  of the world  it is characterized by some of its  teachings and practices like the silent  vicar which  makes it stand out and add to the nuance  and complexity and diversity of  sufism in particular of islam and  religion  in general don't forget to watch  al-mukhadem's video on the history of  islamic presence in tinjan china  it connects to some of the discussions  in this video  you should also definitely subscribe to  his channel it is one of the best  sources for islamic political history  that you can find  online and has some absolutely beautiful  visuals to go along with it i  highly recommend it as always i would  like to extend my  warmest gratitude towards my patrons  without which this channel will not be  possible  especially i would like to extend thanks  to some of my new patrons  manuel fishman and  thank you all so much i would of course  highly appreciate if some  other viewers would like to become  patrons as well this would help me  continue to make these videos i will  leave a link to my patreon page in the  description  you can also of course leave a one-time  donation to my paypal account which i  will also link to  in the description if you want more  videos like this on different suvi  orders then please let me know in the  comments this is one of my favorite  subjects as i'm sure some of you already  know  if you aren't subscribed to the channel  then of course i would highly appreciate  if you would subscribe  and i will see you next time  [Music]  you


